								<div class="title" id="post-title-583102">
																		Decimal places, precision, and variability.
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quikngruvn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(333389); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=333389&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606639688" alt="Quikngruvn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=333389)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quikngruvn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(333389); return false;" >Quikngruvn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1252804514 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Sep 2009 01:15</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-583102">
									

<p>Huh. I didn’t expect to need this thing so soon, but listing 230’s mass to two decimal places is too silly to ignore.</p>
<p>[soapbox]</p>
<p>Adding decimal places to a measurement not only implies a certain level of precision, but also a level of variability in the value of the measurement. Specifically, listing the mass of something to the nearest 0.01&nbsp;kg implies that its mass usually does not fluctuate more than 0.005&nbsp;kg (5&nbsp;g, or <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nickel_%28United_States_coin%29">the mass of a US nickel</a>) above or below the listed value. While I have no doubt that one could get the mass of a person accurate to a fraction of a gram at any given moment, I have a much harder time believing that the mass of any creature with an active metabolism does not significantly fluctuate over the course of a day, week, or year. Heck, a normal person can very quickly (like, in minutes) lose five grams just by sweating - and 230 literally oozes <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">charm</span> ecstasy.</p>
<p>If this were an inanimate object, something with a constant mass, then I’d have no problem if its mass were listed to two or even more decimal places. But the only way such precision would work for the mass of a living thing would be if its mass didn’t change, which (I would think) would be noted somewhere in the article.</p>
<p>I’m just looking for appropriate precision – not every measurement needs to go to three decimal places, and many don’t need any. And a measurement with more decimal places than expected precision would warrant looks… well, silly.</p>
<p>Thank you.</p>
<p>[/soapbox]</p>
<p>Also, 150&nbsp;lb doesn’t seem especially gaunt even for a guy who’s 6-1, but that’s a whole ‘nother debate.</p>

